INT. COLLAPSED MINE SHAFT - SIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

Rock walls dripping. A headlamp on the ground throws a ragged yellow cone. A first-aid kit, half open. A small digital recorder on a flat stone.

LIV ARNESEN, 30s, caked in dust, one leg stretched stiffly in front of her, props herself against the wall. She presses the recorder's red button and holds it close.

LIV
Day two. Or night two. Hard to say. Battery's at twenty percent. Water's gone. I have three granola bars and a flare.

She coughs, long, into her sleeve.

LIV (CONT'D)
My name is Liv Arnesen. If someone finds this, tell my mother I wasn't scared. That's a lie, but tell her anyway.

She clicks it off. Clicks it on.

LIV (V.O.)
(recorded, tinny)
Day one. I'm okay. The ceiling came down fast. The west tunnel's blocked. Fine, I'll try the north.

She listens with her eyes closed. Her recorded voice breaks off with a muffled crash, then silence, then breathing.

LIV
That was the part where I fell.

She clicks it off.

LIV (CONT'D)
The truth is, I keep hearing a drip. And I keep hoping it's a river. But it's just a pipe. A leaking pipe.

She moves her leg. Winces.

LIV (CONT'D)
Okay. Let's make a plan. One. Don't die. Two. Don't panic. Three. If you panic, see one.

A faint scraping sounds from above. She freezes. Waits. Nothing. She lets out a breath.

LIV (CONT'D)
Rats. Probably rats. Or a rescue team with very bad manners.

She laughs. The laugh turns into a sob. She presses the heel of her hand to her mouth until it passes.

LIV (CONT'D)
Sorry. Sorry. Recording. Where was I?

She clicks the button again.

LIV (V.O.)
(recorded, earlier)
If I get out, I'm taking up pottery. Something slow. Something that doesn't fall on me.

Liv smiles at it, tired and cracked.

LIV
You know, that was a good plan.

Overhead, the scraping returns. Louder. A thin shaft of white light falls through a crack in the rock.

LIV (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Hello?

She reaches for the flare, and the rock above her begins to shift.
